    {
      "id": 3858,
      "sender_id": 437,
      "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"308\">∆</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/9-general/topic//near/3855\">schrieb</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>stage2 is still TBD?</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/SAIRcompetition/equational-theories-lean-stage2/blob/main/rules/evaluation.md\">https://github.com/SAIRcompetition/equational-theories-lean-stage2/blob/main/rules/evaluation.md</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Sorry, I do not understand what do you want from me. I can compute polynomials with given Galois groups, but here it becomes very technical for me.</p>\n<p>I hope that I will be able to submit polynomials...</p>\n<p>Let me repeat some earlier remarks. Usually, Galois group computation is not that much depending on the size of the coefficients. In my eyes it is also not too easy to give some help to verify the result. This is com pletely different for checking the field discriminant. Here it would be useful, if someone can provide the field discriminant, or even better the ramified primes. This can be used to improve the running time of the maximal order algorithm...</p>",
